     "rate": "+0%",
     "text": "Ten days on a desk turns draft paper into law without a single signature. You think every new law requires an active presidential stamp to pass, but the system has a hidden automatic timer running in the background. Members of the House or Senate draft, sponsor, and introduce a bill, pushing it through committee debates and formal legislative votes. That bill must pass both the House and Senate in identical form before reaching the president for final review. You expect an official signing ceremony at the desk, but if the president does not sign within ten days, and Congress remains in session, that silent paper becomes binding federal law automatically. In Washington, ten quiet days overrule the president."
    }
   }
